<div style="border: 1px solid #90caf9; background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 5px 8px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #094196;">Group 1: Investment Concepts (Chapters 1-5 | Weightage: 5 Marks) </h3>
</div>

<p style="color: #000; margin-top: 15px;">
This foundational grouping introduces the overarching alternative investment landscape. Candidates must understand the fundamental differences between traditional investments (public equities, standard bonds) and alternative investments (Private Equity, Real Estate, Distressed Assets, Hedge Funds). You will be evaluated on your ability to assess AIF suitability for various portfolio profiles, understanding the concepts of illiquidity premiums and the role of alternative assets in portfolio diversification. </p>


<div style="border: 1px solid #90caf9; background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 5px 8px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #094196;">Group 2: AIF Ecosystem & Structuring (Chapters 6-8 | Weightage: 20 Marks) </h3>
</div>

<p style="color: #000; margin-top: 15px;">
Understanding the structural foundation of a fund is critical. This section delves deeply into the AIF ecosystem. You must possess a comprehensive understanding of the distinct roles and regulatory responsibilities of the Sponsor, the Trustee, the Investment Manager, and the Limited Partners (LPs). Furthermore, candidates will be rigorously tested on the various legal vehicles used for AIF structuring in India, primarily the Trust, the Limited Liability Partnership (LLP), and the Company format. A robust NISM 19C Model Test will evaluate your knowledge of why the determinate trust is the most preferred structure for Category I and II AIFs.</p>





<div style="border: 1px solid #90caf9; background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 5px 8px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #094196;">Group 3: Performance, Benchmarking & Governance (Chapters 9-12 | Weightage: 60 Marks) </h3>
</div>

<p style="color: #000; margin-top: 15px;">
This is the most critical segment of the examination, carrying a massive 60 marks. It is heavily analytical and mathematical. AIF managers must master the unique performance metrics of the private markets. You will face complex calculation-based questions involving the J-Curve effect, Capital Calls (Drawdowns), and distributions. Candidates must flawlessly calculate and interpret the Internal Rate of Return (IRR), the Multiple on Invested Capital (MOIC), Distributions to Paid-In capital (DPI), Residual Value to Paid-In capital (RVPI), and Total Value to Paid-In capital (TVPI).

<br><br>
Equally important in this section is Fund Governance. You must understand conflict of interest management, the role of the Limited Partner Advisory Committee (LPAC), and the strict SEBI mandates regarding co-investments and sponsor commitments. Our NISM Alternative Investment Funds Managers Certification Model Test dedicates an extensive portion of its database to these high-weightage calculations and governance scenarios.</p>





<div style="border: 1px solid #90caf9; background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 5px 8px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #094196;">Group 4: Legal Docs, Valuation & Exit (Chapters 13-15 | Weightage: 30 Marks) </h3>
</div>

<p style="color: #000; margin-top: 15px;">
Private market transactions are governed by dense, bespoke legal contracts rather than standardised exchange rules. This 30-mark module requires candidates to understand the intricacies of the Private Placement Memorandum (PPM), the Contribution Agreement, and the Investment Management Agreement. You must understand how to navigate complex term sheets, including clauses like Liquidation Preferences, Anti-Dilution provisions, Tag-Along/Drag-Along rights, and the intricacies of the Distribution Waterfall (including Catch-up clauses and Carried Interest).

<br><br>
Furthermore, because AIFs invest in unlisted assets, daily market pricing is impossible. You will be evaluated on the International Private Equity and Venture Capital Valuation (IPEV) guidelines. You must understand how independent valuers assess early-stage startups using the VC Method, or mature private companies using Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) and relative multiples. Finally, the unit covers exit mechanisms, including Initial Public Offerings (IPOs), secondary sales, and strategic buyouts. Preparing with comprehensive NISM XIX C Study Materials is essential for mastering this legal and operational terminology.</p>





<div style="border: 1px solid #90caf9; background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 5px 8px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #094196;">Group 5: Taxation & Regulations (Chapters 16-17 | Weightage: 35 Marks) </h3>
</div>

<p style="color: #000; margin-top: 15px;">
Compliance and taxation form the final, formidable hurdle, carrying 35 marks. The taxation of AIFs in India is notoriously complex. Candidates must clearly differentiate between the "pass-through" taxation status granted to Category I and Category II AIFs under Section 115UB of the Income Tax Act, and the fund-level Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) taxation typically applicable to Category III AIFs.

<br><br>

Additionally, you must possess an exhaustive understanding of the overarching regulatory environment. This includes the SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012, as well as the intersection of AIFs with the Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA), the Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA), and the unique regulatory and tax holidays provided to funds operating within the International Financial Services Centre (IFSC) at GIFT City. A highly accurate NISM AIF Managers Mock Test ensures you memorise these vital regulatory thresholds and tax codes flawlessly. </p>






